### Local test

1. Build a 2 GiB raw USB image with `--image` instead of a physical disk.
2. Attach it with a loop device and run the post-build validator.
3. Boot it in QEMU with OVMF.
4. Verify `/etc/sysconfig/tcedir` resolves to the data partition.
5. Verify cached extensions load without external network access.
6. Serve a fixture controller from a local HTTP server and verify it executes
   exactly once even if both boot hooks are invoked manually.
7. Reboot and verify `RUN_ONCE=1` prevents a second run.
8. Delete the success flag, block HTTP, reboot, and verify the fallback runs.
9. Boot the same image with SeaBIOS and repeat persistence checks.
10. Corrupt one cached extension and verify post-build validation fails.

### Physical-machine test

1. Use a dedicated USB with no valuable data.
2. Record its serial with `lsblk -o NAME,SERIAL,SIZE,MODEL`.
3. Build the USB and type the serial confirmation.
4. Boot a disposable PC in UEFI mode with all internal disks disconnected.
5. Confirm Tiny Core obtains networking, loads packages, runs the controller
   once, and stores logs on `TINYDATA`.
6. Reboot and confirm persistence and run-once behaviour.
7. Repeat in legacy BIOS mode if the target hardware supports it.
8. Disconnect networking, clear the success flag, and verify fallback execution.
9. Mount `TINYDATA` on the build workstation and inspect all expected state and
   log files.

### Completion evidence

- UEFI and BIOS QEMU console logs.
- Physical boot logs from `TINYDATA`.
- `build-manifest.json` matching the tested USB.

## Phase 2 — Refactor the supervisor state controller and enforce safety

### Goal

Turn `check-so/autoinstall-ubuntu.sh` into a testable state machine that can
inspect and repair safely without accidentally selecting an excluded disk.

### Code tasks

- [ ] Split the controller into:

  ```text
  check-so/autoprov-controller.sh
  check-so/lib/common.sh
  check-so/lib/config.sh
  check-so/lib/disks.sh
  check-so/lib/os-state.sh
  check-so/lib/grub-state.sh
  check-so/lib/boot-order.sh
  check-so/lib/state-machine.sh
  check-so/lib/installer-launch.sh
  ```

- [ ] Keep `autoinstall-ubuntu.sh` temporarily as a compatibility wrapper.
- [ ] Define commands: `inspect`, `plan`, `apply`, and `report`.
- [ ] Make `inspect` and `plan` read-only and usable without destructive flags.
- [ ] Make `apply` default to `DRY_RUN=1`.
- [ ] Add overridable `SYS_ROOT`, `DEV_ROOT`, and command wrappers so unit tests
  use fixtures rather than host `/sys` and `/dev`.
- [ ] Replace undefined `err` calls with one tested error function.
- [ ] Parse configuration without executing arbitrary shell code.
  - [ ] Replace sourced `installer.env` with strict `KEY=VALUE` parsing.
  - [ ] Reject unknown keys and invalid booleans, UUIDs, URLs, and versions.
- [ ] Build a normalized disk inventory containing kernel name, canonical path,
  major/minor, size, removable, read-only, transport, rotational flag, serial,
  WWN, filesystem UUIDs, PARTUUIDs, mounts, and parent relationships.
- [ ] Implement exclusion once and reuse it everywhere.
  - [ ] Exclude the live-media parent disk.
  - [ ] Exclude UUID and PARTUUID matches.
  - [ ] Exclude mounted disks and the running root disk.
  - [ ] Exclude removable disks unless specifically selected for a test.
  - [ ] Never bypass exclusions in a fallback selector.
- [ ] Replace numeric bonus scoring with deterministic ordered fields:
  explicit serial, explicit UUID, NVMe, nonrotational, size, stable name.
- [ ] Require exactly one safe target before any state-changing action.
- [ ] If multiple OS roots exist, report all and require `CHECK_UUID`.
- [ ] Handle no OS root as a first-class state; never call `mount` with an empty
  source.
- [ ] Mount probes into unique `mktemp` directories and always unmount via trap.
- [ ] Detect encrypted, LVM, mdraid, ZFS, and unsupported layouts and return
  `manual_intervention`, not `empty_disk`.
- [ ] Parse `/etc/os-release` without leaking its variables globally.
- [ ] Compare exact OS ID, release, architecture, and optional build policy.
- [ ] Check both GRUB source configuration and generated configuration.
- [ ] Prevent duplicate `ds=nocloud` arguments when repairing GRUB.
- [ ] Compare `config_version` content with requested version.
- [ ] Define state-machine outputs:
  `ready`, `needs_config`, `needs_grub_repair`, `needs_install`,
  `ambiguous`, `unsafe`, and `failed`.
- [ ] Emit a JSON report and a human-readable report for every run.
- [ ] Identify the installed Ubuntu UEFI boot entry by disk/partition GUID and
  set `BootNext`; do not reorder an arbitrary first entry.
- [ ] Add a global lock to prevent concurrent controller executions.
- [ ] Remove all ignored failures from required operations.

### Local test

1. Add Bats fixtures for SATA, virtio, NVMe, MMC, removable USB, read-only disk,
   multiple roots, excluded UUID, excluded PARTUUID, LVM, and empty disk.
2. Test every state-machine input and expected output.
3. Test that every excluded-disk scenario returns `unsafe` and never calls a
   mocked write command.
4. Test partition-path generation for all supported device families.
5. Test GRUB repair twice and verify the second run makes no change.
6. Test version values `missing`, `older`, `equal`, `newer`, and malformed.
7. Create sparse QCOW2 disks with fixture filesystems and run `inspect` against
   them through QEMU/NBD in an isolated CI job.
8. Run `apply` without flags and verify it only prints a dry-run plan.

### Physical-machine test

1. Boot the Tiny Core USB with all internal disks disconnected; expect
   `unsafe/no target` and no changes.
2. Connect one blank disposable disk; verify `inspect` returns `needs_install`.
3. Add an exclusion matching that disk; verify it returns `unsafe`.
4. Install a fixture Ubuntu manually, omit the configuration marker, and verify
   `needs_config`.
5. Remove the NoCloud GRUB argument and verify `needs_grub_repair`.
6. Add a second disk with another OS and verify the controller refuses automatic
   selection.
7. Capture before/after partition-table hashes during all read-only tests and
   verify they are unchanged.

### Completion evidence

- Unit-test matrix covering every state.
- JSON reports from physical scenarios.
- Proof that excluded and ambiguous disks were not modified.

