## 4. Recommended implementation order

Do not parallelize destructive or architecture-defining work before the shared
contracts exist. The safe order is:

1. Phase 0: tooling and schemas.
2. Phase 1: deterministic USB.
3. Phase 2: safe state controller.
4. Phase 3: Ubuntu installation.
5. Phase 4: first boot and Ansible foundation.
6. Phase 5: PXE services.
7. Phase 6: role selection and menus.
8. Phase 7, Phase 8, and Phase 9: client roles. These can be developed in
   parallel only after Phase 6 is accepted.
9. Phase 10: continuous pull.
10. Phase 11: CI/CD acceleration.
11. Phase 12: optional Terraform.
12. Phase 13: TestBench repair/integration; this may be done earlier by a
    separate contributor because it is isolated from the provisioning path.
13. Phase 14: final operational hardening and release.

## 5. Definition of complete system

The project is complete when all of the following are demonstrated:

- The USB boots on supported UEFI hardware and loads persistent configuration.
- It never selects an excluded, mounted, ambiguous, or wrong-serial disk.
- It installs Ubuntu 24.04 on a registered supervisor using the official
  installer.
- First boot converges through Ansible and writes an accurate version marker.
- The supervisor serves PXE only on the isolated provisioning network.
- Known MACs receive their assigned role; unknown MACs boot locally.
- NAS, Proxmox, and edge installations target only registered disk serials.
- Every host converges periodically through a locked, rollback-capable pull.
- CI validates changes in staging and only accelerates approved pull execution.
- Secrets are absent from Git and logs.
- Every destructive path has automated negative tests and a physical disposable
  hardware test.
- The complete environment can be rebuilt and recovered using the documented
  runbooks.
